.\" bluetooth(8) — NextBSD Bluetooth command-line client.
.\"
.\" NextBSD-original. There is no upstream page to vendor: Apple's equivalent
.\" surface lives inside the closed bluetoothd. This page is written for this
.\" tool.
.\"
.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt BLUETOOTH 8
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm bluetooth
.Nd control Bluetooth
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm sdp
.Ar address
.Nm
.Cm power
.Cm on | off
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm discoverable
.Cm on | off
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm scan
.Op Fl t Ar seconds
.Op Ar node
.Nm
.Cm pair
.Ar address
.Op Ar pin
.Nm
.Cm confirm
.Ar address
.Cm yes | no | Ar passkey | Ar pin
.Nm
.Cm unpair
.Ar address
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the command-line client for
.Xr bluetoothd 8 ,
the NextBSD Bluetooth daemon.
.Pp
It is a pure-C Mach client: it looks up the
.Ql org.nextbsd.bluetooth
Mach service and issues MIG RPCs.
It does not link CoreFoundation or SystemConfiguration, so it is also the
supported way for anything above the Mach line, including the Gershwin
desktop, to drive Bluetooth.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "status"
.It Cm status
Print the daemon's state, the persisted policy (power, discoverable,
auto-connect), the number of paired devices, and each controller with its
transport, USB vendor and product, state, and, once it has been brought up,
its address and name.
.It Cm list
Print the paired devices from the pairing store: address, name, class of
device, link
.Po
.Ql BR/EDR ,
.Ql LE
or
.Ql dual
.Pc ,
whether it reconnects automatically, its connection state, and the profiles
it offers.
.It Cm sdp Ar address
Browse the SDP records of the Classic (BR/EDR) device at
.Ar address ,
paired or not, and print every attribute of every record.
.Xr bluetoothd 8
connects to the device, which may page it, reads the records and renders
them; well-known service classes, protocols and attributes are named.
This replaces
.Ql sdpcontrol browse .
.It Cm power Cm on | off Op Ar node
Power a controller on or off; with no
.Ar node ,
the first one.
Off disconnects every device and stops the controller answering pages and
inquiries.
The choice is saved as
.Ql ControllerPowerState
and applies to controllers that arrive later.
Bluetooth is on by default.
.It Cm discoverable Cm on | off Oo Fl t Ar seconds Oc Op Ar node
Let other devices find this computer by inquiry.
With
.Fl t
it turns itself off again after
.Ar seconds
and the setting is not saved; without, it is saved as
.Ql DiscoverableState .
.It Cm scan Oo Fl t Ar seconds Oc Op Ar node
Discover nearby devices for
.Ar seconds
(default 10, at most 60), then print each one's address, name, class and
signal, and whether it is paired.
Names come from the device's extended inquiry response, the daemon's cache,
or a name request after the inquiry.
Prints
.Dq no devices found
when there were none.
.It Cm pair Ar address Op Ar pin
Pair with a device and follow the pairing to the end.
If the device asks for confirmation, a passkey or a PIN,
.Nm
asks on the terminal; without a terminal it prints what is needed and exits
with status 3, and
.Cm confirm
answers.
A keyboard shows nothing to confirm: type the passkey
.Nm
prints on the keyboard, then press Enter on it.
Headphones and speakers pair with no question.
A legacy device that needs a PIN can be given it here.
.It Cm confirm Ar address Cm yes | no | Ar passkey | Ar pin
Answer the prompt a pairing with
.Ar address
is waiting on:
.Cm yes
or
.Cm no
for a number to compare, the six digits a device shows for passkey entry,
or the PIN.
A prompt not answered within 60 seconds fails the pairing.
.It Cm unpair Ar address
Forget a paired device: its keys, its entry in the pairing store, and its
link if it is connected.
.El
.Pp
Addresses print as
.Ql AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF ;
an LE random address carries a
.Ql /random
suffix.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/" -compact
.It Pa /usr/sbin/bluetooth
This command.
.It Pa /var/log/bluetoothd.stderr
The daemon's log.
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.plist
(policy and paired devices, 0644) and
.Pa org.nextbsd.Bluetooth.keys.plist
(link keys and LE keys in plaintext, 0600).
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 2 -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
An error, or
.Xr bluetoothd 8
could not be reached.
.It 2
Usage error.
.It 3
.Cm pair
needs an answer and there is no terminal to ask on; use
.Cm confirm .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bluetooth status
state:         active
power:         on
discoverable:  off
auto-connect:  on
paired:        1

controller:    ubt0
  transport:   USB 8087:0a2b
  state:       on
  address:     A4:34:D9:01:02:03
  name:        thinkpad-t460s
  powered:     on
  discoverable: off
  features:    SSP LE EIR

$ bluetooth scan
scanning for 10 seconds...
ADDRESS                  NAME                         CLASS           SIGNAL       PAIRED
B8:D5:0B:12:34:56        Beats Studio                 headphones      ***  -58     no

$ bluetooth pair B8:D5:0B:12:34:56
paired with B8:D5:0B:12:34:56

$ bluetooth list
ADDRESS                  NAME                     CLASS  LINK     AUTO STATE        PROFILES
C8:2E:47:11:22:33/random MX Keys                  000000 LE       yes  connected    HOGP

$ bluetooth sdp 00:1B:66:AA:BB:CC
record 0x00010001: AudioSink
  0x0000 ServiceRecordHandle: 0x00010001
  0x0001 ServiceClassIDList: { 0x110b (AudioSink) }
  0x0004 ProtocolDescriptorList: { { 0x0100 (L2CAP), 0x0019 }, { 0x0019 (AVDTP), 0x0103 } }
  0x0009 BluetoothProfileDescriptorList: { { 0x110d (AdvancedAudioDistribution), 0x0103 } }
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "bluetooth: no Bluetooth adapters"
No controller is present, or none has been brought up yet.
.It "bluetooth: adapter is powered off"
Turn it on with
.Ql bluetooth power on .
.It "bluetooth: cannot reach org.nextbsd.bluetooth — is bluetoothd running?"
The Mach service lookup failed.
Check
.Ql launchctl list | grep bluetoothd
and
.Pa /var/log/bluetoothd.stderr .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bluetoothd 8 ,
.Xr scutil 8 ,
.Xr wlan 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in NextBSD in 2026.
